### What is the noisy-neighbor problem?

Every multi-tenant system shares something: a database connection pool, a thread pool, a message queue, CPU, or memory. Sharing is what makes multi-tenancy economical. The problem is that shared resources have no built-in fairness.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

public class NoisyNeighborDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int totalThreads = 20;
        int bigCorpRequests = 18; // one tenant grabs 18 of 20 threads
        int threadsLeftForEveryone = totalThreads - bigCorpRequests;

        System.out.println("Shared thread pool: " + totalThreads);
        System.out.println("BigCorp grabs: " + bigCorpRequests + " threads (bulk payments)");
        System.out.println("Threads left for Rajesh, Priya, and 10,000 others: "
                + threadsLeftForEveryone);
        System.out.println("Everyone else's transfers are starved.");
    }
}
```

```text
Shared thread pool: 20
BigCorp grabs: 18 threads (bulk payments)
Threads left for Rajesh, Priya, and 10,000 others: 2
Everyone else's transfers are starved.
```

### How does per-tenant rate limiting work?

A token bucket per tenant caps how many requests per second each tenant may perform. A burst from one tenant is throttled at its own bucket.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

import com.google.common.util.concurrent.RateLimiter;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class PerTenantRateLimit {

    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, RateLimiter> limiters =
            new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    static boolean tryAcquire(String tenantId) {
        RateLimiter limiter = limiters.computeIfAbsent(
                tenantId, t -> RateLimiter.create(100.0)); // 100/s per tenant
        return limiter.tryAcquire();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Rajesh (small tenant): " + tryAcquire("rajesh"));
        System.out.println("BigCorp (exceeding quota): " + tryAcquire("bigcorp"));
        System.out.println("BigCorp's burst is throttled at ITS bucket, not everyone's");
    }
}
```

```text
Rajesh (small tenant): true
BigCorp (exceeding quota): true
BigCorp's burst is throttled at ITS bucket, not everyone's
```

### How does bounded concurrency per tenant work?

A semaphore per tenant caps how many requests that tenant may have in flight at once. A tenant with many slow requests blocks only itself.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;

public class PerTenantConcurrency {

    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Semaphore> permits =
            new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    static boolean tryProcess(String tenantId) {
        Semaphore sem = permits.computeIfAbsent(tenantId, t -> new Semaphore(10));
        if (!sem.tryAcquire()) {
            System.out.println(tenantId + ": REJECTED (max concurrency reached)");
            return false;
        }
        try {
            System.out.println(tenantId + ": processing (permits left: "
                    + sem.availablePermits() + ")");
            return true;
        } finally {
            sem.release();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        tryProcess("bigcorp");
        tryProcess("rajesh"); // Rajesh unaffected by BigCorp's concurrency
    }
}
```

```text
bigcorp: processing (permits left: 9)
rajesh: processing (permits left: 9)
```

### How do separate pools (bulkheads) provide physical isolation?

Dedicate thread pools or queues per tenant tier. A flood on the free-tier pool cannot touch premium tenants.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class TenantBulkhead {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExecutorService premiumPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(15);
        ExecutorService standardPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
        ExecutorService bulkPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);

        System.out.println("Premium pool: 15 threads (Rajesh, Priya)");
        System.out.println("Standard pool: 10 threads (regular tenants)");
        System.out.println("Bulk pool: 5 threads (BigCorp batch jobs)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("BigCorp floods bulk pool → only bulk pool saturated");
        System.out.println("Premium and standard pools: UNAFFECTED");

        premiumPool.shutdown();
        standardPool.shutdown();
        bulkPool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
Premium pool: 15 threads (Rajesh, Priya)
Standard pool: 10 threads (regular tenants)
Bulk pool: 5 threads (BigCorp batch jobs)

BigCorp floods bulk pool → only bulk pool saturated
Premium and standard pools: UNAFFECTED
```

### How do you compare the isolation strategies?

| Strategy | Isolation Level | Cost | Complexity | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Per-tenant rate limit | Logical | Low | Low | Baseline for all tenants |
| Bounded concurrency | Logical | Low | Low | Preventing thread pool exhaustion |
| Separate pools per tier | Physical | Medium | Medium | Distinct tenant tiers (premium/standard) |
| Workload isolation | Physical | Medium | Medium | Interactive vs bulk workloads |
| Dedicated infrastructure | Complete | High | High | Largest/most sensitive tenants |

### How do you decide the right level of isolation?

It is a product and economics decision, not a purely technical one. Weigh four factors.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

public class IsolationDecision {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Factor 1: Blast radius");
        System.out.println("  Payment path → strong isolation. Reporting → soft quotas.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Factor 2: Tenant value and SLA");
        System.out.println("  Premium (guaranteed latency) → dedicated pool.");
        System.out.println("  Free tier → shared with soft quotas.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Factor 3: Cost");
        System.out.println("  Dedicated infra multiplies spend. Apply selectively.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Factor 4: Operational complexity");
        System.out.println("  More pools = more to monitor and scale.");
    }
}
```

```text
Factor 1: Blast radius
  Payment path → strong isolation. Reporting → soft quotas.

Factor 2: Tenant value and SLA
  Premium (guaranteed latency) → dedicated pool.
  Free tier → shared with soft quotas.

Factor 3: Cost
  Dedicated infra multiplies spend. Apply selectively.

Factor 4: Operational complexity
  More pools = more to monitor and scale.
```

### What are common mistakes?

Just adding threads when one tenant overloads the pool. The next bigger burst grabs those too.

```java
package com.bank.multitenant;

// WRONG: "BigCorp is starving everyone? Add more threads!"
// Next month: BigCorp sends an even bigger burst, grabs the new threads too
// No fairness → no isolation → same problem at higher scale

// CORRECT: per-tenant quota + bounded concurrency as baseline
// BigCorp's burst is contained at its own allocation
// Other tenants are unaffected regardless of burst size
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you detect a noisy neighbor in production monitoring?
- What is the difference between rate limiting and concurrency limiting?
- When does regulation require dedicated infrastructure per tenant?
- How do you handle a tenant that legitimately needs more capacity?
